1
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,300
Welcome to the Cinema Museum. My name's Jon Davies and I'm a volunteer here and I teach film

2
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:23,440
studies here as well. This is an amazing building. It's the old workhouse from 1871 and it's

3
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:29,360
what remains of it. So what makes this special is that it's the place where Charlie Chaplin

4
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,320
and his brother Sidney and his mum came when they fell on poor times. And Chaplin came back

5
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:38,500
several times later to see his mother once he got out of the workhouse. But it's been the

6
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:44,020
home of the Cinema Museum in Kennington now for many years and we have a collection here

7
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:52,400
of amazing things about going to cinema, seats, uniforms, costumes, signs, posters, stills.

8
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:56,520
It's just amazing. And we have lots and lots of screenings here and this afternoon we're

9
00:00:56,580 --> 00:01:01,760
showing a film and in the evenings we show silent films and people come here and it's a

10
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:06,300
wonderful place and we hope that it's going to have a great future. So perhaps come inside

11
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:14,520
and have a look. For many film museums around the world, concentrate on the films themselves.

12
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:18,980
This is a museum that has a great deal more about going to the pictures, going to the

13
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:23,460
cinema, what it was like and how cinemas worked. So I'm surrounded by projectors

14
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:28,580
that go right back to the early days of cinema, the early days of silent films and sound films.

15
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:34,340
So these are extraordinary relics now of a time when we projected film. Nowadays everything's

16
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:42,740
digital. And also the old signs that you'd see outside a cinema telling you whether the

17
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:51,220
cinema was full, whether they're still seats, how much the seats were. And also very

18
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:58,280
importantly the certificates that the films had. So this is a particularly amazing one.

19
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:02,620
This is from a time when we still had a category H for horror, which is something

20
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:09,540
that even I don't remember. So this is a collection that's been built up over the

21
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:13,640
years of things that would otherwise have just been thrown away. And they're a memory

22
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:18,280
of the great working class art of the 20th century cinema. It's just utterly beautiful.

23
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:27,340
This is cinema usherette's uniform from the 1930s. It was for the majestic Aberdeen

24
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:35,300
run by the Donalds family. They were a family firm who had the 13 cinemas in

25
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:41,360
Aberdeen at their height. They only have Her Majesty's now, which is mostly a

26
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:47,200
theatre. But uniforms were terrifically common. All cinema chains had different

27
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:53,280
uniforms. Even small independent cinemas they would be wearing a uniform. They lasted

28
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:57,160
till about the 1960s and then slowly got phased out.

29
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:05,720
Okay, so this is my museum's collected things that otherwise would just

30
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:10,920
become vague memories. And so it's keeping part of history alive that is

31
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:14,740
really important to understanding what happened in the 20th century. So for

32
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:19,800
instance, the usherettes' torches, the special signs for the children's shows,

33
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:24,040
and these the spray cans that they'd spray on the audiences to keep the place

34
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:27,840
smelling nice at a time when people didn't bath quite so much, didn't change

35
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:32,420
their clothes quite so much, and smoked a great deal. We've also got some weird

36
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:38,340
stuff like collections of cinema teapots, which somebody's given us. So that's

37
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:43,180
kind of amazing. And then the cinemas themselves, if you look along here,

38
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:49,180
would actually design their own seats. And here's a variety of seat rows from

39
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,780
chains of cinemas. So it's really quite amazing. And then some of the lobby

40
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:59,480
photos that would have attracted people in to see their favourite film stars.

41
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:06,880
And here we've got some of the very earlier sound film equipment. At a time

42
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,880
when you used to play the sound from a gramophone record, you'd run the film

43
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,360
on the projector and run the gramophone record alongside it. It caused all

44
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:20,860
sorts of problems if the gramophone record slipped, if the film had lost a few

45
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:24,940
frames, everything went out of sync. So it wasn't a system that lasted too long.

